.SH "NAME"
udevd \- Device event managing daemon
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
udevd
.HP \w'\fB/sbin/udevd\fR\ 'u
\fB/sbin/udevd\fR [\fB\-\-daemon\fR] [\fB\-\-debug\fR] [\fB\-\-ready\-notify=\fR] [\fB\-\-children\-max=\fR] [\fB\-\-exec\-delay=\fR] [\fB\-\-event\-timeout=\fR] [\fB\-\-resolve\-names=early|late|never\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBudevd\fR
listens to kernel uevents\&. For every event, udevd executes matching instructions specified in udev rules\&. See
\fBudev\fR(7)\&.
.PP
The behavior of the daemon can be configured using
\fBudev.conf\fR(5), its command\-line options, environment variables, and on the kernel command\-line, or changed dynamically with
\fBudevadm control\fR\&.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-daemon\fR
.RS 4
Detach and run in the background\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-debug\fR
.RS 4
Print debug messages to standard error\&. The log level set with
\fIudev\&.log_priority=\fR
on the kernel command line takes precedence over it\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-ready\-notify=\fR
.RS 4
Issue (S6/dinit compatible) readiness notification via a file descriptor\&. A short message followed by a newline character will be sent via the specified file descriptor (inherited from the parent process) when initialization is complete\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-children\-max=\fR
.RS 4
Limit the number of events executed in parallel\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-exec\-delay=\fR
.RS 4
Delay the execution of
\fIRUN\fR
instructions by the given number of seconds, or by a time span with a unit, e\&.g\&.
500ms\&. This option might be useful when debugging system crashes during coldplug caused by loading non\-working kernel modules\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-event\-timeout=\fR
.RS 4
Set the number of seconds to wait for events to finish\&. After this time the event will be terminated\&. The default is 180 seconds, which is also used when 0 is given\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-resolve\-names=\fR
.RS 4
Specify when udevd should resolve names of users and groups\&. When set to
\fBearly\fR
(the default), names will be resolved when the rules are parsed\&. When set to
\fBlate\fR, names will be resolved for every event\&. When set to
\fBnever\fR, names will never be resolved and all devices will be owned by root\&.
.RE
.PP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
.RS 4
.RE
.SH "KERNEL COMMAND LINE"
.PP
Parameters starting with "rd\&." will be read when
\fBudevd\fR
is used in an initrd, and are ignored otherwise, also by
\fIIMPORT{cmdline}\fR
in the rules\&. As in systemd,
\fBudevd\fR
considers itself running in an initrd when
/etc/initrd\-release
exists\&. As initrds that are not made for systemd do not provide
/etc/initrd\-release, unlike systemd it also does so when the root file system is a memory file system (the rootfs of an initramfs, i\&.e\&. ramfs or tmpfs),
/init
exists, and one of the files that such initrd generators put into the initrd exists:
/conf/initramfs\&.conf
(initramfs\-tools),
/lib/dracut\-lib\&.sh
(dracut before version 019),
/init_functions
(old versions of mkinitcpio),
/etc/initrd\&.defaults
(genkernel before version 4\&.2) or
/\&.modloop
(mkinitfs of Alpine Linux)\&. The detection can be overridden with the
\fI$SYSTEMD_IN_INITRD\fR
environment variable, set to a boolean value\&.
.PP
In the names of the parameters, "\-" and "_" are the same, e\&.g\&.
\fIudev\&.children\-max=\fR
is the same as
\fIudev\&.children_max=\fR\&.
.PP
\fIudev\&.log_priority=\fR, \fIrd\&.udev\&.log_priority=\fR
.RS 4
Set the log level\&. It takes precedence over
\fIudev_log=\fR
in
\fBudev.conf\fR(5)
and over
\fB\-\-debug\fR, and
\fBudevadm control \-\-log\-level=\fR
takes precedence over it\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIudev\&.trace[=\fR\fI\fIBOOL\fR\fR\fI]\fR, \fIrd\&.udev\&.trace[=\fR\fI\fIBOOL\fR\fR\fI]\fR
.RS 4
Enable/disable trace logging\&. When enabled,
\fIudev\&.log_priority=\fR
will be ignored, and
debug
level is assumed\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIudev\&.children_max=\fR, \fIrd\&.udev\&.children_max=\fR
.RS 4
Limit the number of events executed in parallel\&. It takes precedence over
\fB\-\-children\-max=\fR, unless it is 0\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIudev\&.exec_delay=\fR, \fIrd\&.udev\&.exec_delay=\fR
.RS 4
Delay the execution of
\fIRUN\fR
instructions by the given number of seconds, or by a time span with a unit, e\&.g\&.
500ms\&. This option might be useful when debugging system crashes during coldplug caused by loading non\-working kernel modules\&. It takes precedence over
\fB\-\-exec\-delay=\fR, unless it is 0\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIudev\&.event_timeout=\fR, \fIrd\&.udev\&.event_timeout=\fR
.RS 4
Wait for events to finish up to the given number of seconds\&. This option might be useful if events are terminated due to kernel drivers taking too long to initialize\&. It takes precedence over
\fB\-\-event\-timeout=\fR, unless it is 0\&.
.RE
.PP
\fInet\&.ifnames=\fR
.RS 4
Network interfaces are renamed to give them predictable names when possible\&. It is enabled by default; specifying 0 disables it\&.
.RE
.PP
\fInet\&.naming_scheme=\fR
.RS 4
Selects the naming scheme, i\&.e\&. the version of the naming logic of the
\fBnet_id\fR
builtin, that is used to generate the predictable names of network interfaces\&. It takes the name of a scheme:
eudev
for the names generated by
\fBnet_id\fR
of eudev 3\&.2\&.15 and older, one of the schemes of systemd
v238,
v239,
v240,
v241,
v243,
v245,
v247,
v249,
v250,
v251,
v252,
v253,
v254,
v255,
v257,
v258,
v259,
v260
and
v261, or
latest
for the latest one, which is the default\&. The schemes are described in
\fBudev\fR(7)\&. This option takes precedence over
\fInet_naming_scheme=\fR
in
\fBudev.conf\fR(5)\&. The
\fI$NET_NAMING_SCHEME\fR
environment variable of
\fBudevd\fR
takes precedence over both, unless its value starts with
:\&.
.RE
.PP
For testing, the kernel command line can be replaced by the value of the
\fI$UDEV_PROC_CMDLINE\fR
environment variable of
\fBudevd\fR
and
\fBudevadm\fR, when it is set, even to an empty string\&. It applies to the options described here, to the
\fInet\&.ifnames=\fR
option used by
80\-net\-name\-slot\&.rules, and to
\fIIMPORT{cmdline}\fR
in the rules in general, and it is handled like the kernel command line, e\&.g\&. options starting with "rd\&." are ignored outside of an initrd:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
UDEV_PROC_CMDLINE="net\&.naming_scheme=v250" udevadm test\-builtin net_id /sys/class/net/eth0
UDEV_PROC_CMDLINE="net\&.ifnames=0" udevadm test /sys/class/net/eth0
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.PP
For compatibility with systemd,
\fI$SYSTEMD_PROC_CMDLINE\fR
is used the same way when
\fI$UDEV_PROC_CMDLINE\fR
is not set\&.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBudev.conf\fR(5),
\fBudev\fR(7),
\fBudevadm\fR(8)
